  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... After a frost advisory this morning, temperatures \nwill be above normal through mid-week. \n\nHigh pressure will dominate over the region for the next several \ndays. With calm surface winds through this morning, there will be \ngood radiational cooling conditions through the night. This will \nallow for temperatures to dip into generally the mid to upper 30s \nwith low 40s in the south and urban areas late tonight through early \nmorning. Thus, a Frost Advisory remains in effect until 8 AM this \nmorning for much of central NC, besides the far SE counties. \n\nMaximum temperatures this afternoon and tomorrow will rise up to 10 \ndegrees above normal. This equates to highs in the low to mid 70s \neach afternoon. Today is likely to be a degree or two warmer than \ntomorrow as low level thicknesses are slightly lower tomorrow \nafternoon. Minimum temperatures will also rise each night, with lows \ngenerally expected in the low to mid 40s late tonight and in the mid \n40s to around 50 Wednesday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A moisture-starved clipper low and frontal system \nwill provide a chance of light rain Thu and a step down to below \naverage temperatures for Friday through mid next week.\n\nA mid/upr-level cyclone will develop over srn ON/QC and the Great \nLakes late this week and progress slowly across and offshore the \nNortheast and Atlantic Canada through the weekend, during which time \na series of shortwave perturbations will progress through the \nassociated cyclonic flow across and offshore the Middle Atlantic.\n\nAt the surface, a clipper low will be in the process of deepening \nacross the Middle Atlantic Thu and/or redeveloping (\"transferring \nenergy\") to an initial coastal low off the the coast of the Middle \nAtlantic and srn New England. A related, trailing cold front will \nprogress across cntl NC on Thu. A closely-spaced, reinforcing cold \nfront (related to the next in the aforementioned series of shortwave \nperturbations) will follow and likely move across cntl NC late Fri-\nFri night. High pressure will follow and build from central Canada \nto the Middle Atlantic this weekend through early next week, where \nit will likely move little while gradually weakening through the \nmiddle of next week.  \n\nAlready minimal QPF with the moisture-starved clipper low and frontal \nsystem on Thu has trended lower and lees than a tenth of an inch \nthroughout cntl NC. In fact, only a fleeting moist/saturated layer \nin the mid-levels, atop a dry sub-cloud layer below ~800 mb suggest \nrainfall amounts will generally be several hundredths of an inch, if \nany across srn zones. \n\nIt will otherwise trend much cooler, with the coldest air likely to \nfollow the reinforcing cold front into the region late Fri-Fri \nnight. Point forecast soundings depict relatively steep low-level \nlapse rates in CAA behind that front for Sat morning, which would \nlimit further cooling potential via radiative processes. The coldest \ntemperatures will likely follow as that CAA abates and the MSL \npressure gradient relaxes by Sun and Mon morning, when sub-freezing \ntemperatures appear likely in all but perhaps the major urban areas. \nA hard freeze will be possible especially in outlying/rural Piedmont \nlocations. While some moderation will result as the high gradually \nweakens through the middle of next week, its continued presence and \ninfluence will yield continued below average temperatures and \nmultiple, consecutive mornings of damaging cold.",
